menu "router_queue"

    config ROUTER_WORK_QUEUE_SIZE
        int "Work queue (bytes)"
        default 4096
        range 1024 32768
        help
          Ring buffer of normal commands waiting for the work task: each takes
          its request, a 16-byte header (answer callback, its ctx, origin) and
          the ring's own header. Multiple of 4.

    config ROUTER_URGENT_QUEUE_SIZE
        int "Urgent queue (bytes)"
        default 1024
        range 256 8192
        help
          Ring buffer of commands flagged urgent. Multiple of 4.

    config ROUTER_WORK_TASK_PRIO
        int "Work task priority"
        default 5
        range 1 24

    config ROUTER_WORK_TASK_STACK
        int "Work task stack (bytes)"
        default 6144
        range 3072 32768
        help
          Runs the module routers of normal commands and their answer
          callbacks (the session's framer).

    config ROUTER_URGENT_TASK_PRIO
        int "Urgent task priority"
        default 15
        range 1 24
        help
          Above every task that may run a long command, so an urgent one
          (stop) never waits behind it.

    config ROUTER_URGENT_TASK_STACK
        int "Urgent task stack (bytes)"
        default 6144
        range 3072 32768
        help
          Runs urgent commands and router_run_urgent (the safe-state
          handler on a dead-man).

    #@limit @alias Calls to other boards
    config ROUTER_CALLS
        int "Calls in flight"
        default 16
        range 1 64
        help
          This board's calls out of the router's port waiting for an answer
          (router_request); a call to this board takes none. Every call out is
          answered, so each write of a remote device in flight takes one. A
          slot is about 52 B.

    #@limit @alias Call timeout @unit ms
    config ROUTER_CALL_TIMEOUT_MS
        int "Call timeout (ms)"
        default 1000
        range 50 10000
        help
          A call without an answer this long ends with
          ERR_ROUTER_CALL_TIMEOUT, unless the caller gives its own. Longer than
          a radio's tail, so a slow answer is not taken for a lost one.

endmenu
